# Resumé

Picture it. **Fifteen years ago**, there I was -- stitching together HTML tags -- taming the [T. rex][trex] that roamed the *infobahn.*

Today I prefer bullet trains. **Ruby on Rails** has been my focus since 2007 -- and the pace of innovation is *unyielding*.  It's fantastic!

## Work History

The following positions encompass my career as a **developer**. Forays into project management (as indicated) have served to reinforce *my passion to create.* To be a developer -- to learn, to build, to share.

### Jobber (October 2012 - present; Edmonton, AB)

Jobber is a new business management application targeted at field service companies. We endeavour to be a one-stop shop, offering invoicing, scheduling and a variety of (industry specific) add-ons. Thus far I have worked on the timesheets add-on, performance optimizations, and 

Tools:

* Ruby on Rails 3.x, Postgres, JQuery/Mobile, Git, Heroku

### Freelance

While I was "in-between things", a graphics designer and I built a WordPress theme for [Dr. Winnifred Tang](http://learn2manageld.com/). I also read _Bulletproof Design_ and _Responsive Design._

### Yardstick, Testing and Training Experts  (July 2009 - May 2012; Edmonton, AB)

Yardstick Measure is a multi-tenant **learning management system** (LMS) focused on creating and delivering proctored exams. Our core team fluctuated from 2-4 developers. We collaborated with whiteboards, code reviews, and pair programming. I was responsible for creating a booking system and adding e-commerce support (including taxation). I also scripted multiple data migrations from third-party systems. In 2011, I accepted additional **project management** responsibilities, managing the tasks of our core team and several external contractors. I also served as the primary contact for a significant client, with offices in Montreal and Geneva. Still, I kept on coding, as visible in this [code_swarm](https://vimeo.com/35538422) of the project.

Tools:

* Ruby on Rails 2.x, MySQL, MongoDB, Redis, JQuery, Git, GitHub, Airbrake, Lighthouse/FogBugz (2.5 years) 
* ColdFusion, Microsoft SQL Server (legacy LMS, 5 months)

### Power to Change Ministries (May 2007 - May 2009; Langley, BC)

In April 2007, I was able to _raise funds_ for a summer internship that piqued my interest. The project enabled **human resources** to create forms that applicants could complete online. What became a ten month project was primarily a solo-developer effort, with some assistance from Campus Crusade for Christ U.S.

Global Aid Network (GAiN) is the humanitarian arm of Power to Change. For a start, we launched a new web site, with the help of a graphics designer from the marketing team.

At GAiN, my primary task was to provide a tool to better manage **water well sponsorship** data, which at the time was kept in _one giant Excel file._ Using technologies prescribed by IT, I developed a custom database that integrated with donor/donation data. Now at over 400 wells, I can't imagine maintaining all that data in Excel.

Tools:

* Ruby on Rails 1.2, MySQL, Prototype.js, Subversion, Trac (10 months) 
* WordPress (PHP/MySQL), JQuery, Slideshow Pro, Mercurial (2 months) 
* Microsoft Access (VBA), Microsoft SQL Server, Microsoft Reporting Services (5 months)

### Freelance (February - March 2007; Edmonton, AB)

While looking for work, I attained [certification in PHP 5](http://yng.mn/zendcert5). Much to my delight, I landed my first two contracts with Ruby on Rails in 2007: a new app for Royer Homes and fixing up Econolution.

Tools:

* Ruby on Rails 1.2, MySQL (2 months)

### Wycliffe Bible Translators (September - November 2006; Calgary, AB)

Having an interest in Biblical languages and Wycliffe's mandate, I attended a *summer of linguistics* program at CanIL. After finishing school, I _volunteered_ with Wycliffe Canada, where I worked on two small projects. The first, a personnel data browser for IT, the other, a tool to format job opportunities into PDFs. I enjoyed the experience, but unfortunately I couldn't volunteer with Wycliffe forever.

Tools:

* Django, Python, PostgreSQL, LDAP (2 months)

### Vintacom Media Group (December 2002 - May 2006; Edmonton, AB)

Through participation in a local ColdFusion User Group, I was offered a job in the **online dating** industry. The multi-tenant platform had large datasets, high-traffic, massive mail-outs, and thousands of financial transactions every day. Each of our 4-5 developers specialized in different areas to some extent. Early on I was entrusted with implementing additional payment gateways, renewals, and better fraud controls.

In 2005, I accepted additional **project management** responsibilities, but left in the fall for a four month _sabbatical_ at **Tauernhof Bible School** in Austria. Returning to Vintacom the following January, it was during this time period that I became interested in Ruby on Rails, and attended the Canada on Rails conference.

Tools:

* ColdFusion, Microsoft SQL Server, IIS, CVS, Userplane (3 years)

### Sagebrush Corporation (June 1999 - December 2002; Edmonton, AB)

Following the merger of several related companies, I was brought on to design a new web site, incorporating a searchable catalog for Econo-Clad’s rebound books. This was my first foray into **e-commerce,** and an important transition from mostly static brochure-ware sites to fairly sophisticated web apps (including a portal site and an internal task management tool). My immediate manager was the only other web developer, but we worked among half-a-dozen software developers, whom I was able to glean a lot from. It was also cool that they fed my Apple fanboyism with a ticket to WWDC!

Tools:

* ColdFusion, Microsoft SQL Server, IIS, Visual Source Safe/CVS, Fireworks (3.5 years)

### Canadiana Group (November 1997 - June 1999; Edmonton, AB)

This **design firm** is where I was introduced to Photoshop, to slice and optimize graphics from our in-house designers. There were 3-4 developers, but we tended to work independently on different projects. Though most web sites were static HTML, we did use client-side and server-side scripting, and even a few databases.

Tools:

* Cold Fusion 3.1, Microsoft Access, JavaScript, Photoshop (1.5 years)

### Pathways Data Systems (June - November 1997; Edmonton, AB)

My entry into **web development** was in **1997**, working part-time on several small web sites. On the side, I developed _Hyperprocess_, my own static-site generator that supported file includes and other helpful functions (written in C).

Tools:

* HTML 3.2, Paint Shop Pro (5 months)

## Pastime

E-books and tech talks complement courses in computer programming (NRI 1994), linguistics (CanIL 2006) and photography (TWU 2008). To **share** what I'm learning, I frequent [local meetups][yegrb], hackathons and code retreats.

Taking transit keeps me active, and I enjoy hiking and soccer when it's *not freezing* outside, or tea by the fireplace when it is.
 
